 ;; ── evolve ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 :batch
 {:id "kotobase-cost-kaizen-3"
  :members ["h8-l2-cache-api" "h7-parse-only-addressable-values"]
  :order "h8 first, then h7"
  :why "They buy DIFFERENT resources -- h8 the 84 class-B gets, h7 the CPU of
        parsing every value -- so the pair is the sum, not the max. h8 goes
        first because it is :S, reversible, semantically inert, and needs no
        credential and no write to the graph."
  :pair-cost-per-million 20.46
  :pair-saving 51.24
  :h1-is-a-choice-not-an-order
  "h1 is the largest single number and is deliberately NOT first: it SUBSUMES
   h8 rather than adding to it (both remove the same reads), it is :L, and its
   maintenance is a rebuild per commit whose cost depends on a read:write ratio
   nothing has measured."
  :h4-is-superseded-on-this-graph
  "Packing's whole saving here is the same 84 gets h8 removes, and it removes
   fewer of them (84->64: each novelty cell is its own commit and therefore its
   own pack). After h8 it is worth close to nothing on THIS graph. Packing earns
   its place on the WRITE path of the other Worker -- where a fold re-PUTs every
   unchanged tree node under `if-none-match: *` and reads each one back to
   byte-compare it -- and that is not a resource this judge prices."}

 ;; ── landed this iteration ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 :landed
 {:what "h8-l2-cache-api"
  :where "net-kotobase/control-plane, main 57b1f350f"
  :branch "agent/hydrate-recost-20260818"
  :files ["protocols-worker/src/kotobase_protocols_worker/kotobase_r2.cljs"
          "protocols-worker/src/kotobase_protocols_worker/kotobase_store.cljs"
          "protocols-worker/src/kotobase_protocols_worker/worker.cljs"
          "protocols-worker/test/kotobase_protocols_worker/kotobase_store_test.cljs"]
  :tests {:baseline "130 tests, 821 assertions, 5 failures (all ipni_test)"
          :with-change "131 tests, 828 assertions, 5 failures -- the same five"
          :broken "l2-get never consults the cache -> 7 failures: 2 in the new
                   test, 0 collateral"
          :restored "back to 5"}
  :the-instrument-is-half-the-landing
  "`{:cache-hits n}` cannot distinguish a tier that works from a tier that is
   present and never hit, and the second costs an extra lookup per block while
   saving nothing. The diagnostic now counts where the answer CAME FROM --
   blocks_from_l1 / blocks_from_l2 / blocks_from_r2 -- plus `l2_available`,
   because a zero under a runtime with no Cache API means NOT ASKED. The old
   split was derived from an `is it in L1?` pre-check and would have counted
   every L2 hit as an R2 read: the one number that decides whether the tier is
   worth having."
  :blocks-unattributed
  "New, and not always zero. The derived form hid that by construction
   (`from-r2` was `gets - hits`, absorbing whatever was left whether or not R2
   was reached). Two real sources: a second caller joining an in-flight fetch,
   and an absent block. A number that always balances proves nothing."
  :not-yet-deployed
  "The release build was still waiting on the same build governor when this
   receipt was written. Landed on main is not deployed, and the hit rate cannot
   be measured until it is."}

